\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A saddle of FitzHugh–Nagumo}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Take the FitzHugh–Nagumo velocity of \ref{fnip-0018} with #{I=0},
#{b=0.01}, and #{a=c=0.1}. An equilibrium satisfies
#{w=(b/c)V=V/10} and #{V(a-V)(V-1)=w}. One solution is the origin.
Any further equilibrium has nonzero voltage and satisfies
##{V^{2}-(a+1)V+\bigl(a+\tfrac{b}{c}\bigr)=0.}
For these parameter values that quadratic has positive discriminant,
so there are two further equilibria. The origin is a stable
[focus](fnip-0035). One of the other two is a [saddle](fnip-0035),
and the remaining one is a stable [node](fnip-0035).}

\p{Figure 4.22 of \citet{p. 109}{izhikevich2007dynamical} uses these
parameters. \citet{§4.3.1, p. 108}{izhikevich2007dynamical} records two
stable equilibria separated by an unstable equilibrium. The stable
and unstable manifolds of the saddle are the separatrices of that
figure. In the drawing below, the
dotted segments are the eigenlines of the saddle, and the solid curves
through the open circle are the [local stable manifold](fnip-0044)
and the [local unstable manifold](fnip-0044). Arrows on the local
stable manifold point toward the saddle. Arrows on the local unstable
manifold point away from it, one branch to the focus and one to the
node.}

